From 4c4a058b02f99135d19221232c4f0adca47a1855 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 15:21:51 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0315 -- fine-grained timing is off unless
 DOSAGS_TICKSTATS_FINE=1

WHY: this port's per-phase, event-type and render timers read uclock() -- a PIT read over port I/O --
at every scope. On MMD that was ~89 reads a game tick on the 486, in every build, and in situ each
scope (the read, a 64-bit multiply and divide, the stats update) costs far more than the read alone.
A same-binary 486 ABBA (dosags LAB-MMD.md 45.3) measured turning them off: logic -0.691 ms/tick
(N 0.031), update_game_once -0.897, the steady tick rate 35.2 -> 36.4 ticks/s. With them off, MMD's
logic read 24.69 ms/tick in both cells, under the full-speed line. They are a measurement tool, so
players should not pay for them.

WHAT:
- Every DosPortTickScope, the event-type scopes (event.cpp), the rep_exec/script_vm timers
  (script.cpp) and the fast-blit timers (ali3dsw.cpp) now time only when fine timing is on.
- DOSAGS_TICKSTATS_FINE=1 turns it on. The value is read once in ags_entry_point into a global, so
  each scope tests one flag; the default is off.
- Three scopes are marked coarse and always time: update_game_once_total, render_to_screen and
  scene_construct. Logic (update_game_once minus render minus scene) is computed from them, so it is
  reported the same way whatever the setting.
- DosTickStallScope (the stall share), journeys' timestamps and 0220's pump stamps are unchanged.
- The benchmark harness sets DOSAGS_TICKSTATS_FINE=1 by default (run-hw.sh, run-dosbox.sh), so every
  existing per-phase figure keeps its meaning. A cell measuring what a player runs opts out with
  --no-fine-timing.

Checked (LAB-MMD 45.2, 46.5):
- With fine timing off, only the coarse phases record. The harness default records all 44.
- Game state is identical with fine timing on and off on MMD, AGS Quest and 5 Days; the perturbed
  control differs.
- The ratchet gives identical verdicts, except exe-bytes and code-lock-bytes, which grow by the switch's
  ~80 gated sites (the ceilings are raised with this patch, pre-approved).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
